Q: Multi-responsibility model method in CodeIgniter is not performing in accordance with parameter type and quality rules A: <p>Your problem with this script is the number 1 reason why having a multi-responsibility model method is a bad idea. Method Swiss Army knife can do so many things that it has become difficult to manage.</p>
<p>The call site of this script doesn't consistently cast numeric strings as integers, so your model method is performing unexpectedly.</p>
<p>I strongly recommend a refactor.  Create separate methods which have a clear behavior and can be easily maintained and unit tested.</p>
<p>These methods should atomic (do only one thing), have a single parameter with a single datatype, and have a narrow return type.</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>public function getByUserId(int $id): ?object
{
    return $this-&gt;db
        -&gt;join(
            &quot;{$this-&gt;tableUserDetails['table']} ud&quot;,
            &quot;u.{$this-&gt;tableUser['column']['id']} = ud.{$this-&gt;tableUserDetails['column']['id_user']}&quot;
        )
        -&gt;get_where(
            &quot;{$this-&gt;tableUser['table']} u&quot;,
            [&quot;u.{$this-&gt;tableUser['column']['id']}&quot; =&gt; $id]
        )
        -&gt;row();
}

public function getByUserName(string $userName): ?object
{
    return $this-&gt;db
        -&gt;join(
            &quot;{$this-&gt;tableUserDetails['table']} ud&quot;,
            &quot;u.{$this-&gt;tableUser['column']['id']} = ud.{$this-&gt;tableUserDetails['column']['id_user']}&quot;
        )
        -&gt;get_where(
            &quot;{$this-&gt;tableUser['table']} u&quot;,
            [&quot;u.{$this-&gt;tableUser['column']['username']}&quot; =&gt; $userName]
        )
        -&gt;row();
}

public function getUserGroupId(string $groupName): ?int
{
     return $this-&gt;constantsServices[$param][$groupName] ?? null;
}

public function getUsersByGroupId(int $groupId): array
{
    return $this-&gt;db
        -&gt;join(
            &quot;{$this-&gt;tableUserDetails['table']} ud&quot;,
            &quot;u.{$this-&gt;tableUser['column']['id']} = ud.{$this-&gt;tableUserDetails['column']['id_user']}&quot;
        )
        -&gt;get_where(
            &quot;{$this-&gt;tableUser['table']} u&quot;,
            [&quot;u.{$this-&gt;tableUser['column']['id_user_groups']}&quot; =&gt; $groupId]
        )
        -&gt;result();
}
</code></pre>
<p>Additional tips:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>select('*')</code> can be safely omitted; it is the default.</li>
<li><code>get_where()</code> can be used to consolidate <code>where()</code>, <code>from()</code>, and <code>get()</code>.</li>
<li>A controller or library should be used to determine which model methods are appropriate for any given scenario.</li>
</ul>

Q: Where should I allocate the pool in Python multiprocessing? A: <p>While the previous comments and answers were very helpful, it took me still quite some time to make them work for me. The main reason was that I had oversimplified the task in my original post. The task &quot;poolswimmer&quot; in my application is a class that contains data along with the processing functions. This worked very well when starting it as a <em>threading.Thread</em>, but when I made it a process, I ran into problems.</p>
<p><em>multiprocessing.Pool</em> transports the data to the referent by pickling the complete class, including the methods, and that fails for security reasons.</p>
<p>I have solved this by handcrafting the transport of the data only with JSON content, like in this example:</p>
<pre><code>from cpool import CPool
import multiprocessing as mp
import multiprocessing.managers as m
import json

class Strange:
    def __init__(self, lst):
        self._lst = lst

    def __str__(self):
        return f&quot;{[itm for itm in self._lst]}&quot;

class Packed:
    def __init__(self):
        self._num = 0
        self._txt = &quot;&quot;
        self._flt = 0.0
        self._lst = Strange([])

    def populate(self, jdata):
        self._num = jdata['num']
        self._txt = jdata['txt']
        self._flt = jdata['flt']
        self._lst = Strange(jdata['lst'])

    def prt(self):
        print(&quot;Content:&quot;)
        print(f&quot;num: {self._num}, txt: {self._txt}, flt: {self._flt}, lst: {self._lst}&quot;)

    @classmethod
    def prepare_for_json(cls, num, txt, flt, lst):
        JData = {'num': num, 'txt': txt, 'flt': flt, 'lst': lst}
        return json.dumps(JData)

    @staticmethod
    def execute(jdat):
        target = Packed()
        target.populate(json.loads(jdat))
        target.prt()

if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:
    myp = CPool()
    targ1 = Packed.prepare_for_json(27, &quot;Jo mei&quot;, 17.5, [2, 3, 4, 6])
    targ2 = Packed.prepare_for_json(42, &quot;So isses&quot;, 17.6, [2, 9, 4, 6])
    t1 = myp.apply_async(Packed.execute, targ1)
    t2 = myp.apply_async(Packed.execute, targ2)
    myp.join()
</code></pre>
<p>The class CPool that I use here is the following:</p>
<pre><code>&quot;&quot;&quot;
    class CPool
    provides a pool for compute bound processes
&quot;&quot;&quot;
class CPool:
    def __init__(self, processes=os.cpu_count()):
        self._procs = [None for i in range(processes)]
        self._sentinels = []
        self._queue = mp.Queue()
        self._lock = mp.Lock()
        self._monitor = None

    def apply_async(self, func, *args):
        self._lock.acquire()
        if self._procs.count(None) == 0:
            self._queue.put((func, args))
            self._lock.release()
            return False
        else:
            self.start(func, *args)
            if not self._monitor:
                self._monitor = th.Thread(target=self.monitor.__func__, args=(self,))
                self._monitor.start()
            return True

    def start(self, _func, *_args):
        ind = self._procs.index(None)
        self._procs[ind] = mp.Process(target=_func, args=_args)
        self._procs[ind].start()
        self._sentinels.append(self._procs[ind].sentinel)
        self._lock.release()

    def monitor(self):
        while len(self._sentinels) &gt; 0:
            fins = mp.connection.wait(self._sentinels)
            self._lock.acquire()
            i = 0
            for proc in self._procs:
                if proc and not proc.is_alive()::
                    proc.join()
                    self._procs[i] = None
                    self._sentinels.remove(proc.sentinel)
                i += 1
            self._lock.release()
            while not self._queue.empty():
                func, args = self._queue.get()
                if not self.apply_async(func, *args):
                    break
        self._lock.acquire()
        self._monitor = None
        self._lock.release()

    def join(self):
        if self._monitor:
            self._lock.acquire()
            mon = self._monitor
            self._lock.release()
            mon.join()
        if not self._queue.empty():
            print(&quot;CPool error: jobs pending&quot;)
        if len(self._sentinels) &gt; 0:
            print(&quot;CPool error: jobs running&quot;)
        for proc in self._procs:
            if proc:
                print(&quot;CPool error: job alive&quot;)
</code></pre>
<p>It works well in my application, which has the following characteristics:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Its purpose is to process data in chunks, where the processing can be divided in an I/O bound part and a compute bound part.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Each of the parts takes several minutes of processing time, so the overhead for an extra compute process for each chunk can be tolerated.</p>
</li>
<li><p>The data flow from the I/O bound part and from there into files, there isn’t any feedback.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Exceptions in the compute bound should not prevent the processing of the following chunks.</p>
</li>
</ul>
